		'option_d' => 'Scope of practice refers only to ethics',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => 'Why A is correct: The course separates legal authority from individual readiness. Scope of practice concerns what a license or registration legally permits. Scope of competence concerns whether this clinician has sufficient education, training, experience, supervision, consultation, and skill to provide the service safely.\n\nWhy B is incorrect: Both concepts apply across practice settings. Private practice and agency employment may create different procedures, but the distinction between legal scope and personal competence remains relevant in either setting.\n\nWhy C is incorrect: An insurer may impose coverage or credentialing requirements, but it does not establish a clinician\'s professional competence. Competence is evaluated through the clinician\'s preparation, experience, support, and ability to practice safely.\n\nWhy D is incorrect: Scope of practice is a legal and regulatory concept, not merely an ethical one. Ethical standards may reinforce the duty to remain competent, but they do not replace the legal scope established for the profession.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Ask two separate questions: Is the service legally within my professional scope, and am I personally prepared to provide it competently?\n\nSource Alignment: Module 1; course Objective 1.',
