	array(
		'question_text' => 'Which statement best reflects informed consent?',
		'option_a' => 'It is completed once during intake and never revisited',
		'option_b' => 'It is primarily a billing requirement',
		'option_c' => 'It is an ongoing process of communication, clarification, and agreement',
		'option_d' => 'It applies only to high-risk clients',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => 'Why A is incorrect: A signed intake form does not end informed consent. Consent should be revisited when services, risks, fees, technology, communication methods, treatment units, or other important conditions change.\n\nWhy B is incorrect: Fees are one part of informed consent, but the process is broader. It includes the nature of services, risks, benefits, alternatives, confidentiality, policies, boundaries, and opportunities for questions.\n\nWhy C is correct: The course teaches informed consent as an ongoing professional conversation. The clinician explains relevant information, checks understanding, clarifies expectations, responds to changes, and documents the process.\n\nWhy D is incorrect: Informed consent applies to routine and high-risk care. The amount and detail may vary with the service and circumstances, but it is not limited to clients presenting with elevated risk.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: A form records consent; communication creates and maintains informed consent.\n\nSource Alignment: Module 2; course Objective 2.',
