		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => 'Why A is incorrect: A clinician\'s belief that disclosure might be helpful does not by itself create legal authority. The clinician still needs authorization or another recognized legal basis.\n\nWhy B is incorrect: A parent\'s relationship to an adult client does not automatically authorize disclosure. The clinician must evaluate the adult client\'s authorization and any applicable legal exception.\n\nWhy C is incorrect: Emotional upset is clinically important, but it does not by itself create a confidentiality exception.\n\nWhy D is correct: When the facts meet the legal threshold for a mandated report, the reporting duty creates a recognized exception to the ordinary confidentiality rule. The clinician should report to the legally designated recipient, limit unnecessary disclosure, document, and continue clinical follow-up.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Disclosure requires authority. A legal reporting threshold—not pressure, preference, or discomfort—creates the duty.\n\nSource Alignment: Final Module 3 and Module 4 reporting content; course Objectives 3 and 4.',
