		'explanation' => 'Why A is incorrect: The course does not teach a universal rule requiring family members to be warned first. Protective actions depend on the applicable legal framework, the facts, and the person or agency that must be contacted.\n\nWhy B is incorrect: Anger alone does not automatically establish a serious threat or require hospitalization. The clinician assesses seriousness, specificity, credibility, victim identifiability, access, context, and level of care.\n\nWhy C is correct: Within the course framework, California’s duty-to-protect statute is triggered only in the limited circumstance in which the patient has communicated to the psychotherapist a serious threat of physical violence against a reasonably identifiable victim or victims. The precise response depends on current California law and the facts.\n\nWhy D is incorrect: Discussion of danger does not automatically authorize release of the entire record. Protective disclosure and record production are distinct legal questions, and disclosure should be limited to valid authority and purpose.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Assess the threat and the legal trigger; do not convert every angry statement into automatic warning, hospitalization, or record release.\n\nSource Alignment: Module 5; course Objective 5. Verified against Civil Code section 43.92 on July 27, 2026.',
