		'explanation' => 'Why A is incorrect: Immediate warning may be required after the legal and clinical threshold is established, but the statement alone does not provide enough information to determine the appropriate protective action.\n\nWhy B is incorrect: Law-enforcement contact may be part of a protective response, but it is premature before clarifying the threat unless the available facts already indicate an immediate emergency.\n\nWhy C is correct: The therapist should assess what was said, target identifiability, intent, plan, means, access, preparation, timeframe, history, escalation, credibility, protective factors, and immediate safety. Assessment then informs consultation and proportionate action.\n\nWhy D is incorrect: Termination does not assess or manage the threat and could disrupt care during a high-risk period.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Assessment comes before selecting the protective response, unless the existing facts already require immediate emergency action.\n\nSource Alignment: Module 5; course Objective 5.',
