		'explanation' => 'Why A is incorrect: Records must not be altered, backdated, rewritten, or disguised after a complaint or investigation begins. Any legitimate later entry must follow an accurate amendment or late-entry process.\n\nWhy B is incorrect: Board correspondence may contain deadlines and required actions. Ignoring it can compound the problem.\n\nWhy C is correct: The clinician should identify what is requested and by when, preserve records, notify the malpractice carrier when appropriate, obtain qualified licensing or legal consultation, and respond professionally through the required process.\n\nWhy D is incorrect: Directly pressuring the complainant may be retaliatory, coercive, counterproductive, or confidentiality-breaching. The complaint should be handled through formal channels.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Preserve, understand, consult, and respond—never alter, ignore, or retaliate.\n\nSource Alignment: Module 6; course Objective 6.',
