		'explanation' => 'Why A is incorrect: Urgent action may sometimes be necessary, but the framework rejects impulsive action without gathering the information needed to understand the risk and legal duty.\n\nWhy B is correct: The sequence structures clinical judgment: pause, assess risk, clarify facts and authority, consult when needed, take protective action proportionate to the situation, and document the reasoning and follow-up.\n\nWhy C is incorrect: Warning before assessment can lead to unnecessary disclosure or the wrong intervention. Assessment and clarification identify whether a protective duty is triggered and what response fits.\n\nWhy D is incorrect: Crisis situations vary. Some require emergency services; others may be managed through safety planning, increased contact, consultation, support involvement, voluntary evaluation, or another proportionate response.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Use a consistent process so urgency does not become impulsivity.\n\nSource Alignment: Module 5 and course capstone framework; course Objective 5.',
