		'explanation' => 'Why A is incorrect: The course explicitly avoids reducing discipline to one catastrophic event. The case was used to examine how concerns across records, billing, advertising, license status, communication, termination, and cooperation can form a pattern.\n\nWhy B is incorrect: Documentation supports client care; it does not outrank it. The lesson is that clinical and administrative responsibilities work together to protect clients and professional accountability.\n\nWhy C is correct: The corrected course teaching is that multiple professional concerns may combine into a pattern that results in serious discipline. Small failures become more significant when they repeat, interact, or remain uncorrected.\n\nWhy D is incorrect: Investigations are not inevitable. Preventive systems, timely response, accurate records, consultation, and professional follow-through can reduce risk and support an appropriate response if a complaint occurs.\n\nCTA Clinical Takeaway: Look for patterns early. Repeated small breakdowns can create a larger professional problem.\n\nSource Alignment: Module 6 corrected script; course Objective 6. Final case-source verification remains part of the separate prepublication review.',
